<Base title="Concepts — Catalog of Complexity" description="Why Kolmogorov complexity isn't a column, and other conceptual notes.">
  <h1 class="page-title">The conceptual layer: why Kolmogorov complexity isn't a column</h1>
  <p class="page-lede">
    Complexity science's most celebrated formal measures — Kolmogorov complexity, logical
    depth, thermodynamic depth, effective complexity — are theoretically central to the field
    and almost entirely absent from this catalog's 30 columns. That is a deliberate editorial
    call, not an oversight.
  </p>

  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      The panel only records what can honestly be filled for real macroscopic non-biological
      systems: a published or defensibly derived value, or an anchored 0&ndash;4 judgment, with
      its evidence stated. The famous deep measures of complexity fail that test for a structural
      reason, not a laziness one — a dedicated research pass (<code>research/phase1/formal-measures.md</code>)
      checked each one against the pilot roster (the Sun, Hurricane Katrina, Earth's climate, the
      San Andreas Fault, the Mississippi basin, New York City, the NYSE, the Internet, ERCOT,
      English) and found no verified numeric value for any of them, for any real non-biological
      system, anywhere in the literature that pass could reach. So instead of a column, each
      measure gets an honest explanation of why it stayed out — the "conceptual layer" below.
    </p>

    <h2>Kolmogorov complexity</h2>
    <p>
      Kolmogorov (algorithmic) complexity is the length of the shortest program that outputs a
      given string on a fixed universal machine. It is formally uncomputable — a direct
      consequence of the halting problem, not a data-availability gap — so no citation will ever
      supply a "true" value for any system, no matter how well instrumented. Real-world work
      instead uses compression-based proxies such as normalized compression distance, but the
      best-attested large-scale applications of that proxy are genomic (whole-genome phylogeny,
      protein classification) — biological, and outside this catalog's scope. No comparably
      well-cited application to a macroscopic non-biological system's native data (a compressed
      grid topology, a hydrograph) was found. It remains the conceptual root that several other
      measures on this list approximate, worth a mention, not a column.
    </p>

    <h2>Logical depth</h2>
    <p>
      Bennett's logical depth measures how long a near-optimally compact description takes to
      unfold into the object it describes — deep objects have short descriptions but long causal
      histories. It inherits Kolmogorov complexity's uncomputability and adds a second layer
      (finding a program within a tolerance of the shortest one, itself an undecidable problem).
      No confirmed instance of logical depth being numerically computed for any real macroscopic
      system — physical, geophysical, or socio-economic — turned up in the research pass; the
      literature uses it qualitatively, as an illustrative thought experiment, not as a measured
      quantity. It is philosophically resonant with this project's interest in path-dependence
      and historical process, but there is no number to put in a cell.
    </p>

    <h2>Thermodynamic depth</h2>
    <p>
      Lloyd &amp; Pagels proposed thermodynamic depth as the entropy of the ensemble of possible
      histories that could have led to a system's current state. It requires a specified
      partition of the system into macroscopic states plus the transition dynamics between them —
      and that partition has never been given a principled, non-subjective definition. Shalizi's
      documented critique treats the measure as fundamentally unworkable for exactly this reason:
      Lloyd and Pagels never specify how the macrostates are supposed to be chosen. No real-system
      numeric value has been found. Statistical complexity, below, was developed in part to fix
      this exact problem by replacing ad hoc macrostates with causal states.
    </p>

    <h2>Effective complexity</h2>
    <p>
      Gell-Mann &amp; Lloyd's effective complexity tries to isolate the algorithmic information
      content of a system's <em>regularities</em>, separating structure from residual randomness —
      a purely ordered string and a purely random string both score low; a string with structure
      at many scales scores high. It inherits Kolmogorov complexity's uncomputability and adds a
      further, openly acknowledged problem: the split between "regularity" and "noise" is a
      subjective judgment call with no unique, reproducible answer. No numeric effective-complexity
      value for a real system was found in the literature searched. It is conceptually close to
      what this whole catalog is trying to formalize — non-random structure — which is exactly
      why it is worth naming here even though it cannot be filled.
    </p>

    <h2>Statistical complexity</h2>
    <p>
      This one is different in kind from the measures above: it is usable in principle, with no
      pilot-system values yet — a data gap, not an uncomputability problem. Crutchfield &amp;
      Young's computational mechanics reconstructs the minimal predictive hidden-Markov model (the
      &epsilon;-machine) of a process, and statistical complexity is the entropy of its causal-state
      distribution. It has been computed on real geophysical time series — geomagnetic
      measurements from Halley, Antarctica, and atmospheric turbulence data — so the method is
      real and has been applied to non-biological macroscopic data. But no one has published a
      value for any of this catalog's own systems specifically, and reconstruction is technically
      demanding (alphabet and discretization choices, heavy data requirements, no standardized
      off-the-shelf tool). It is the strongest candidate on this list for eventually becoming a
      derived column, if the catalog ever commissions a fresh reconstruction on, for instance,
      grid load data or a climate index.
    </p>

    <h2>Excess entropy</h2>
    <p>
      Excess entropy (Grassberger's effective measure complexity, later reintroduced as
      "predictive information") is the mutual information between a process's entire past and its
      entire future — a rigorous formalization of "how much does history tell you about what
      comes next." It is well-defined and computable in principle from any sufficiently long time
      series. But Grassberger's own worked numbers are for toy systems — cellular automata,
      iterated maps — and no specific numeric excess-entropy value computed for a named real
      macroscopic system turned up in the search. Partition-dependence (the value changes with
      how a continuous signal is discretized) is an acknowledged, unresolved practical obstacle on
      top of the data gap.
    </p>

    <h2>Multiscale entropy</h2>
    <p>
      Multiscale entropy computes an entropy-rate proxy across a family of coarse-grained versions
      of a time series, producing a curve of entropy against scale rather than a single number.
      It is computationally simple and could technically be applied to any of this catalog's long
      time series. But the deep, heavily-cited literature behind it is almost entirely
      physiological (heart-rate variability, blood pressure, glucose dynamics) — biological, and
      out of scope here — and it outputs a curve rather than a scalar, which does not fit a
      single-value panel column without an extra, non-standardized reduction step (an area-under-
      the-curve summary, for instance). One non-biological climate application was found, but
      without a citable numeric value attached.
    </p>

    <h2>Complexity profile</h2>
    <p>
      Bar-Yam's complexity profile plots the number of independent behaviors visible at a given
      scale as a function of that scale — a genuinely different idea from every other measure
      here, because it is explicitly scale-resolved rather than a single number. It is
      illustrated with schematic curves for idealized regimes and applied in extended prose to
      one case study at a time (most famously the historical trajectory of human civilization),
      but never as a numeric table scored across multiple real systems side by side. The catalog
      treats it as a methodological lens rather than a column: hierarchy_depth, one of the panel's
      30 columns, counts organizational levels, which is a coarse structural cousin of what the
      complexity profile would show as a curve, but it is not the same measurement.
    </p>

    <h2>What did survive into columns</h2>
    <p>
      Two formal measures made it past this filter: <strong>Shannon-type entropy rate</strong> and
      <strong>fractal dimension</strong>. Both are, by a wide margin, the most-computed measures
      on real macroscopic data of everything surveyed. Entropy rate has been estimated for English
      text since Shannon's own 1951 experiment, and more recently for climate records and
      financial tick data, using nothing more exotic than a time series and a symbolization
      scheme — the panel's filling rules require the measurand and discretization convention to
      be named alongside the value, since entropy rate is not invariant to binning choices.
      Fractal dimension has the deepest literature of all: real, decades-old, independently
      published numeric values exist for at least four of the ten pilot systems — the San Andreas
      Fault's trace (D&nbsp;&asymp;&nbsp;1.1&ndash;1.4 depending on segment), river-network geometry,
      urban street networks, and stock-index return series — which is why the panel splits it into
      a spatial and a temporal-correlation column rather than forcing one measurand to do both
      jobs. Both survived for the same reason the others didn't: a real, citable, non-biological
      numeric value exists, not just a compelling theory.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
